WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.613 --> 00:00:16.755
the next morning Ned and Conseil joined me on the platform.

2
00:00:16.775 --> 00:00:22.998
"Well, Sir Naturalist," said Ned, addressing me in a cheerful tone, "where are we today?"

3
00:00:23.018 --> 00:00:27.501
"We're floating on the surface of the Mediterranean Sea," I replied.

4
00:00:27.521 --> 00:00:30.442
"I don't believe that for one minute," said Ned.

5
00:00:30.462 --> 00:00:33.064
"We couldn't have crossed the Isthmus of Suez."

6
00:00:33.972 --> 00:00:37.955
"If Master says it's true, then it must be true," said loyal Conseil.

7
00:00:37.975 --> 00:00:39.876
"Thank you, Conseil."

8
00:00:40.657 --> 00:00:44.819
I described for them how the Nautilus had swiftly passed through Captain Nemo's underwater tunnel.

9
00:00:44.839 --> 00:00:47.541
"That shoreline over there is Egypt.

10
00:00:47.921 --> 00:00:52.885
You have such good eyes, Ned, that you can probably see the Port Said jetty stretching into the

11
00:00:52.965 --> 00:00:55.647
sea." Ned looked in the direction I pointed.

12
00:00:55.667 --> 00:00:59.990
"Why, I believe you're correct, Professor.

13
00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:02.191
We are in the Mediterranean.

14
00:01:02.972 --> 00:01:06.871
Good! Now let's discuss our own business where no one will overhear us."

15
00:01:08.011 --> 00:01:12.014
Ned led the way to a small space behind the electric light on top of the submarine.

16
00:01:12.034 --> 00:01:15.597
"What I have to say is very simple," Ned began.

17
00:01:15.617 --> 00:01:17.418
"We're in Europe now.

18
00:01:18.219 --> 00:01:23.063
Before Captain Nemo's whims drag us back to the Pacific Ocean, I want to leave the Nautilus."

19
00:01:24.164 --> 00:01:28.827
I didn't want to be responsible for restricting the freedom of my companions, but I really had no

20
00:01:28.867 --> 00:01:29.728
desire to leave.

21
00:01:30.752 --> 00:01:36.176
Thanks to Captain Nemo and his submarine, I was rewriting my book on deep-sea life while living in

22
00:01:36.216 --> 00:01:37.077
the very midst of it.

23
00:01:38.057 --> 00:01:40.939
I didn't want to abandon the Nautilus before my research was finished.

24
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.502
"Ned, do you regret the time you've spent underwater?"

25
00:01:44.902 --> 00:01:45.322
I asked.

26
00:01:46.763 --> 00:01:48.945
The harpooner paused for a moment before replying.

27
00:01:50.046 --> 00:01:55.089
Then he crossed his arms and said, "I don't regret it, but I want this voyage to end."

28
00:01:55.109 --> 00:01:58.972
"I have to admit I don't know when that will be," I said.

29
00:01:59.724 --> 00:02:00.604
"But it will end."

30
00:02:00.624 --> 00:02:03.726
"I agree with Master," said Conseil.

31
00:02:03.746 --> 00:02:08.828
"And it's possible that Captain Nemo will let us go after he has crossed every sea on earth."

32
00:02:09.948 --> 00:02:11.249
"I don't think that will happen."

33
00:02:11.649 --> 00:02:12.309
I shook my head.

34
00:02:12.329 --> 00:02:16.571
"We know too much about the captain and his submarine for him to let us go.

35
00:02:17.551 --> 00:02:21.293
But in six months, perhaps we'll find another opportunity to escape."

36
00:02:22.513 --> 00:02:23.514
"Six months!"

37
00:02:24.074 --> 00:02:24.774
exclaimed Ned.

38
00:02:24.794 --> 00:02:27.495
"Where on earth will we be in six months?"

39
00:02:28.536 --> 00:02:29.856
"I don't know," I replied.

40
00:02:29.876 --> 00:02:35.139
"But whenever we decide to escape, we must succeed on our first attempt.

41
00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:41.223
If we fail, we'll never have another opportunity, and Captain Nemo will never forgive us."

42
00:02:42.303 --> 00:02:45.025
Ned was still determined to escape as soon as possible.

43
00:02:46.065 --> 00:02:50.488
He outlined a plan that involved taking the small boat one night when we were along the European

44
00:02:50.548 --> 00:02:53.930
coastline. "Do you want to know what I think of your plan?"

45
00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:54.770
I asked.

46
00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:58.172
"Certainly, Professor Aronnax," said Ned.

47
00:02:59.144 --> 00:03:04.526
"I think we'll never have the chance to escape because Captain Nemo will always be on guard, especially

48
00:03:04.566 --> 00:03:05.387
when we're near Europe."

49
00:03:05.767 --> 00:03:06.227
I stood up.

50
00:03:06.247 --> 00:03:09.228
"And now not another word on the subject.

51
00:03:09.628 --> 00:03:12.670
The day that you're ready to escape, let us know and we'll follow you.

52
00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:14.070
I leave everything up to you."

53
00:03:15.251 --> 00:03:17.231
The next day we reached the Greek islands.

54
00:03:18.692 --> 00:03:23.894
I wanted to spend some time studying the marine life, but the panels in the gallery remained closed

55
00:03:23.954 --> 00:03:24.514
until evening.

56
00:03:25.555 --> 00:03:28.156
After supper Captain Nemo joined me in the gallery.

57
00:03:29.172 --> 00:03:33.433
He seemed preoccupied and didn't speak to me, but kept peering through the windows.

58
00:03:34.293 --> 00:03:36.114
Suddenly a man appeared in the water.

59
00:03:36.134 --> 00:03:37.774
"Look, Captain!"

60
00:03:38.235 --> 00:03:38.575
I cried.

61
00:03:38.595 --> 00:03:40.595
"That poor man has been shipwrecked.

62
00:03:41.175 --> 00:03:41.856
We must save him!"

63
00:03:42.916 --> 00:03:45.316
The captain didn't answer me, but leaned against the window.

64
00:03:46.217 --> 00:03:50.478
To my astonishment, the man in the water approached and pressed his face to the glass.

65
00:03:51.518 --> 00:03:54.679
Captain Nemo gave him a signal and the man headed for the surface.

66
00:03:54.699 --> 00:03:57.980
"Don't worry about him," said Captain Nemo.

67
00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:03.163
"That's Nicholas, a bold diver who swims from one Greek island to the next.

68
00:04:03.563 --> 00:04:04.864
His nickname is 'The Fish.'"

69
00:04:05.985 --> 00:04:08.507
Captain Nemo went over to a chest in a corner of the room.

70
00:04:09.908 --> 00:04:15.152
I could barely contain my shock as he opened it, revealing a supply of gold bars!

71
00:04:16.192 --> 00:04:19.495
He seemed to have forgotten I was there as he carefully counted the bars.

72
00:04:20.195 --> 00:04:22.717
There must have been two tons of gold in that chest.

73
00:04:23.738 --> 00:04:26.199
Where did he get it all and what was he going to do with it?

74
00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:26.940
I wondered.

75
00:04:27.916 --> 00:04:30.757
The captain closed the chest and wrote an address on it in Greek.

76
00:04:31.718 --> 00:04:35.940
He pressed a button to communicate with the crew, and within a few minutes four men appeared.

77
00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:40.361
With great difficulty, they removed the heavy chest from the gallery.

78
00:04:41.382 --> 00:04:42.442
Captain Nemo turned to me.

79
00:04:42.462 --> 00:04:44.543
"What were you saying, Professor?"

80
00:04:44.563 --> 00:04:49.685
"I wasn't saying anything," I replied, still in shock at what I'd witnessed.

81
00:04:49.705 --> 00:04:52.006
"Then I will bid you good night."

82
00:04:52.967 --> 00:04:55.328
I returned to my cabin very puzzled about what I'd seen.

83
00:04:56.808 --> 00:05:01.251
I tried to fall asleep, but I kept wondering about the connection between the appearance of the diver

84
00:05:01.731 --> 00:05:03.733
and the removal of the chest filled with gold.

85
00:05:04.853 --> 00:05:09.016
Soon I felt the rolling movements that signaled the Nautilus had risen to the surface.

86
00:05:10.037 --> 00:05:14.700
I heard steps upon the platform—someone was unfastening the small boat and launching it!

87
00:05:15.641 --> 00:05:18.222
The boat struck the side of the submarine and then all was silent.

88
00:05:19.103 --> 00:05:24.647
Two hours later I heard the same noises and knew that the small boat had returned, and the

89
00:05:24.687 --> 00:05:26.408
Nautilus was sinking below the waves.

90
00:05:27.424 --> 00:05:31.987
The next day I told Ned and Conseil about the strange events of the previous evening.

91
00:05:32.907 --> 00:05:35.108
"How much do you think the gold is worth, Master?"

92
00:05:35.529 --> 00:05:36.189
asked Conseil.

93
00:05:36.209 --> 00:05:38.310
"Where was the gold taken?"

94
00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:39.331
asked Ned.

95
00:05:40.811 --> 00:05:45.114
I had no answer for either question, so I returned to working on my notes in the gallery.

96
00:05:46.094 --> 00:05:50.056
Around five o'clock I began to feel so hot that I took off my jacket.

97
00:05:50.076 --> 00:05:52.057
"Perhaps I'm getting a fever?"

98
00:05:52.498 --> 00:05:52.838
I thought.

99
00:05:53.518 --> 00:05:56.380
I continued my work, but I kept feeling hotter and hotter.

100
00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:58.541
"Could there be a fire onboard?"

101
00:05:59.001 --> 00:05:59.701
I asked myself.

102
00:06:00.742 --> 00:06:03.904
I was about to leave the gallery to check when Captain Nemo entered.

103
00:06:04.624 --> 00:06:05.825
He went over to the thermometer.

104
00:06:05.845 --> 00:06:08.986
"Forty-two degrees," he said.

105
00:06:09.006 --> 00:06:13.009
"If it gets much hotter, we won't be able to stand it," I said.

106
00:06:13.029 --> 00:06:16.190
"Oh, it won't get any hotter if we don't want it to.

107
00:06:16.931 --> 00:06:19.652
We can simply move away from the source that's producing this heat."

108
00:06:20.613 --> 00:06:21.473
"So it's outside?"

109
00:06:21.873 --> 00:06:22.634
I was still puzzled.

110
00:06:23.974 --> 00:06:26.696
"Yes, we're floating in a current of boiling water.

111
00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:31.983
Look!" He opened the panels to reveal a white and boiling sea.

112
00:06:32.003 --> 00:06:36.907
"I wanted you to see the unusual spectacle of an underwater volcano."

113
00:06:37.888 --> 00:06:41.650
I pressed my hand to a window, but the heat was so great that I sprang back.

114
00:06:42.611 --> 00:06:46.234
The Nautilus had stopped moving and the temperature in the room was unbearable.

115
00:06:46.254 --> 00:06:50.056
"We can't stay one moment longer in this boiling water!"

116
00:06:50.437 --> 00:06:50.797
I cried.

117
00:06:50.817 --> 00:06:54.600
"You're right," said Captain Nemo, as calm as ever.

118
00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:58.041
He gave an order and the submarine turned around.

119
00:06:59.022 --> 00:07:01.764
Fifteen minutes later we were breathing fresh air on the surface.

120
00:07:02.724 --> 00:07:07.747
That's when a startling thought occurred to me: if Ned had chosen this part of the Mediterranean for

121
00:07:07.827 --> 00:07:10.188
our escape, we never would have survived.
